MongoDB rules

Design indexes from actual query patterns.

Bound all large reads.

Paginate lists.

Avoid unbounded $in or regex patterns from user input.

Validate GeoJSON.

Use transactions where multi-document consistency is material and supported by the chosen deployment topology.

Preserve event/audit immutability.

6. AWS S3

Use S3 for large objects, not MongoDB.

Store:

Sentinel imagery;

DEM/raster assets;

InSAR products;

change-detection outputs;

evidence photos;

model artifacts;

model reports;

generated documents.

MongoDB stores metadata and object references.

Example:

{
  "source": "sentinel-1",
  "acquisitionDate": "2026-08-29",
  "objectKey": "sentinel-1/aizawl/2026/08/29/product.tif"
}

S3 requirements:

block public access by default;

least-privilege IAM;

encryption at rest;

lifecycle/retention policies where needed;

versioning for critical objects;

access logging/monitoring where appropriate.

7. Redis

