14A. Community Intelligence Architecture

Reporter
  -> Next.js reporting UI
  -> FastAPI upload/report endpoint
  -> validation + rate limiting
  -> private S3 evidence object
  -> MongoDB CommunityReport
  -> enrichment (slope/road/asset proximity)
  -> verification pipeline
  -> confidence / status
  -> public awareness view or authority action queue
  -> Warning Ledger link when an operational event is created

Verification components

Possible signals:

image classification/quality checks;

metadata/timestamp consistency;

geospatial consistency;

rainfall/risk correlation;

satellite/change detection;

InSAR;

sensor evidence;

independent report clustering.

The architecture must support a rules-based baseline before introducing a trained image-verification model. Model outputs are advisory and must not silently authorize public emergency alerts.

Upload security

Store evidence in private S3 buckets.

Never trust client MIME/type alone.

Enforce object-size limits.

Sanitize filenames/object keys.

Generate server-side object identifiers.

Apply malware scanning strategy where required.

Strip or control public exposure of sensitive media metadata.

Use signed access URLs for authorized viewers.

Keep source/provenance metadata separate from public presentation metadata.

Privacy

Collect the minimum reporter identity required for abuse prevention, follow-up, or authorization workflows. Do not expose precise reporter identity publicly by default. Exact coordinates may be generalized in public views where safety/privacy policy requires it.

Duplicate and cluster handling

Use spatial/temporal similarity to identify potential duplicates, but never discard an original report solely because it matches another report. Preserve independent provenance.

